      "text": "To find all integer values of \\(a\\) such that the polynomial \\(x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0\\) has at least one integer root, we will use the Rational Root Theorem. The Rational Root Theorem states that any potential rational root, expressed as a fraction \\(\\frac{p}{q}\\), must have \\(p\\) as a factor of the constant term (here, 7) and \\(q\\) as a factor of the leading coefficient (here, 1). Therefore, the possible rational roots are \\(\\pm 1, \\pm 7\\).\n\nWe will test these possible roots by substituting them into the polynomial and checking if the result is zero.\n\n1. Test \\(x = 1\\):\n   \\[\n   1^3 + 3(1)^2 + a(1) + 7 = 1 + 3 + a + 7 = a + 11\n   \\]\n   For \\(x = 1\\) to be a root, we need \\(a + 11 = 0\\). Solving for \\(a\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   a = -11\n   \\]\n\n2. Test \\(x = -1\\):\n   \\[\n   (-1)^3 + 3(-1)^2 + a(-1) + 7 = -1 + 3 - a + 7 = 9 - a\n   \\]\n   For \\(x = -1\\) to be a root, we need \\(9 - a = 0\\). Solving for \\(a\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   a = 9\n   \\]\n\n3. Test \\(x = 7\\):\n   \\[\n   7^3 + 3(7)^2 + a(7) + 7 = 343 + 147 + 7a + 7 = 497 + 7a\n   \\]\n   For \\(x = 7\\) to be a root, we need \\(497 + 7a = 0\\). Solving for \\(a\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   7a = -497 \\implies a = -71\n   \\]\n\n4. Test \\(x = -7\\):\n   \\[\n   (-7)^3 + 3(-7)^2 + a(-7) + 7 = -343 + 147 - 7a + 7 = -199 - 7a\n   \\]\n   For \\(x = -7\\) to be a root, we need \\(-199 - 7a = 0\\). Solving for \\(a\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   -7a = 199 \\implies a = -\\frac{199}{7}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(-\\frac{199}{7}\\) is not an integer, \\(x = -7\\) is not a root.\n\nFrom the above tests, the integer values of \\(a\\) that make \\(x = 1\\), \\(x = -1\\), or \\(x = 7\\) a root are \\(-11\\), \\(9\\), and \\(-71\\). Therefore, the possible integer values of \\(a\\) are:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-11, 9, -71}\n\\]",
