      "text": "To find all integer values of \\( a \\) such that the polynomial \\( P(x) = x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 \\) has at least one integer root, we start by letting \\( r \\) be an integer root of the polynomial. By the Factor Theorem, \\( r \\) must satisfy the equation \\( P(r) = 0 \\). Therefore, we have:\n\n\\[\nP(r) = r^3 + 3r^2 + ar + 7 = 0\n\\]\n\nThis can be rearranged to express \\( a \\) in terms of \\( r \\):\n\n\\[\na = -\\frac{r^3 + 3r^2 + 7}{r}\n\\]\n\nFor \\( a \\) to be an integer, the expression \\( r^3 + 3r^2 + 7 \\) must be divisible by \\( r \\). This means that \\( r \\) must be a divisor of 7. The divisors of 7 are \\( \\pm 1 \\) and \\( \\pm 7 \\). We will evaluate \\( a \\) for each of these possible values of \\( r \\).\n\n1. If \\( r = 1 \\):\n\n\\[\na = -\\frac{1^3 + 3 \\cdot 1^2 + 7}{1} = -\\frac{1 + 3 + 7}{1} = -\\frac{11}{1} = -11\n\\]\n\n2. If \\( r = -1 \\):\n\n\\[\na = -\\frac{(-1)^3 + 3 \\cdot (-1)^2 + 7}{-1} = -\\frac{-1 + 3 + 7}{-1} = -\\frac{9}{-1} = 9\n\\]\n\n3. If \\( r = 7 \\):\n\n\\[\na = -\\frac{7^3 + 3 \\cdot 7^2 + 7}{7} = -\\frac{343 + 147 + 7}{7} = -\\frac{507}{7} = -\\frac{507}{7} = -72.428571 \\quad \\text{(not an integer)}\n\\]\n\n4. If \\( r = -7 \\):\n\n\\[\na = -\\frac{(-7)^3 + 3 \\cdot (-7)^2 + 7}{-7} = -\\frac{-343 + 147 + 7}{-7} = -\\frac{-193}{-7} = -\\frac{193}{7} = -27.571428 \\quad \\text{(not an integer)}\n\\]\n\nThe only integer values of \\( a \\) that we have found from the possible values of \\( r \\) are \\( -11 \\) and \\( 9 \\). Therefore, the integer values of \\( a \\) such that the polynomial \\( x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0 \\) has at least one integer root are:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-11, 9}\n\\]",
