      "text": "To find all integer values of \\(a\\) such that the polynomial \\(x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = 0\\) has at least one integer root, we start by using the factor theorem. The factor theorem states that if \\(r\\) is an integer root of the polynomial, then the polynomial can be expressed as:\n\n\\[x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = (x - r)(x^2 + bx + c)\\]\n\nExpanding the right-hand side, we get:\n\n\\[x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7 = x^3 + (b - r)x^2 + (c - br + r)x - rc\\]\n\nBy comparing coefficients with the original polynomial \\(x^3 + 3x^2 + ax + 7\\), we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n1. \\(b - r = 3\\)\n2. \\(c - br + r = a\\)\n3. \\(-rc = 7\\)\n\nFrom the third equation, we have:\n\n\\[r = -\\frac{7}{c}\\]\n\nSince \\(r\\) is an integer, \\(c\\) must be a divisor of \\(-7\\), which are \\(\\pm 1, \\pm 7\\). We will consider each case for \\(c\\).\n\n**Case 1: \\(c = 1\\)**\n\nThen \\(r = -7\\). Substituting \\(r = -7\\) into the first equation, we get:\n\n\\[b - (-7) = 3 \\implies b + 7 = 3 \\implies b = -4\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(r = -7\\) and \\(b = -4\\) into the second equation, we get:\n\n\\[c - (-4)(-7) + (-7) = a \\implies 1 - 28 - 7 = a \\implies a = -34\\]\n\nSo, one possible value for \\(a\\) is \\(-34\\).\n\n**Case 2: \\(c = -1\\)**\n\nThen \\(r = 7\\). Substituting \\(r = 7\\) into the first equation, we get:\n\n\\[b - 7 = 3 \\implies b = 10\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(r = 7\\) and \\(b = 10\\) into the second equation, we get:\n\n\\[c - 10(7) + 7 = a \\implies -7 - 70 + 7 = a \\implies a = -70\\]\n\nSo, another possible value for \\(a\\) is \\(-70\\).\n\n**Case 3: \\(c = 7\\)**\n\nThen \\(r = -1\\). Substituting \\(r = -1\\) into the first equation, we get:\n\n\\[b - (-1) = 3 \\implies b + 1 = 3 \\implies b = 2\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(r = -1\\) and \\(b = 2\\) into the second equation, we get:\n\n\\[c - 2(-1) + (-1) = a \\implies 7 + 2 - 1 = a \\implies a = 8\\]\n\nSo, another possible value for \\(a\\) is \\(8\\).\n\n**Case 4: \\(c = -7\\)**\n\nThen \\(r = 